"Matchings in hypercubes extend to long cycles (https://tmuetze.de/papers/match.pdf)\nciteturn96view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 27; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://tmuetze.de/papers/match.pdf\",\"lineno\":114}); Total lines: 2604\nL6@P0: extended to a cycle that visits at least a 2/3-fraction of all vertices.\nL7@P0: 1. Introduction\nL8@P0: Cycles and matchings in graphs are structures of fundamental interest. In this paper, we\nL9@P0: consider these structures in hypercubes, a family of graphs that has been studied widely in\nL12@P0: single element; see Figure 1 (a). It is well-known and easy to show that Qd, d ≥ 2, admits a\nL13@P0: Hamilton cycle, i.e., a cycle that visits every vertex exactly once. Clearly, any Hamilton cycle\nL14@P0: in Qd is the union of two perfect matchings. A matching in a graph is a set of edges that are\nL15@P0: pairwise disjoint, and a matching is perfect if it includes every vertex of the graph.\nL16@P0: 30 years ago, Ruskey and Savage [RS93] asked whether every matching of Qd can be extended\nL17@P0: to a Hamilton cycle. This became known as the Ruskey-Savage conjecture.\nL18@P0: Conjecture 1 ([RS93]). Every matching of Qd, d ≥ 2, can be extended to a Hamilton cycle.\nL19@P0: This problem received considerable attention, and several natural relaxations have been proved.\nL20@P0: In particular, Fink [Fin07] settled the conjecture affirmatively for the case when the prescribed\nL21@P0: matching is perfect, thereby answering a problem due to Kreweras [Kre96]; see Figure 1 (b). In\nL22@P0: fact, Fink established a considerable strengthening, obtained by considering the graph K(Qd),\nL23@P0: which is the complete graph on the vertex set of Qd. In this context, we say that a matching M\nL24@P0: of K(Qd) extends to a Hamilton cycle C (or some other structure) if all edges in C \\ M belong\nL25@P0: to Qd. Put differently, while the matching M may use arbitrary edges of K(Qd), including\nL26@P0: edges not present in Qd, the edges C \\ M used for the extension are required to be edges of the\nL27@P0: hypercube Qd (otherwise C would trivially be present in the complete graph).\nL28@P0: Theorem 2 ([Fin07]). Every perfect matching of K(Qd), d ≥ 2, can be extended to a Hamilton\nL29@P0: cycle.\nL30@P0: In fact, the strengthening to consider K(Qd) instead of Qd is the key idea to Fink’s proof,\nL31@P0: as it makes the induction hypothesis stronger and thus more flexible. This construction\nL32@P0: actually shows that every perfect matching of K(Qd) can be extended to at least 2\nL33@P0: 2\nL34@P0: d−4\nL35@P0: distinct\nL36@P0: (Jiří Fink) Department of Theoretical Computer Science and Mathematical Logic, Charles\nL37@P0: University, Prague, Czech Republic\nL38@P0: (Torsten Mütze) Department of Computer Science, University of Warwick, United Kingdom\nL39@P0: & Department of Theoretical Computer Science and Mathematical Logic, Charles University,\nL40@P0: Prague, Czech Republic\nL41@P0: E-mail addresses: fink@ktiml.mff.cuni.cz, torsten.mutze@warwick.ac.uk.\nL42@P0: An extended abstract of this paper appeared in the Proceedings of IWOCA 2024 [FM24].\nL43@P0: This work was supported by Czech Science Foundation grant GA 22-15272S. Both authors participated in\nL44@P0: the workshop ‘Combinatorics, Algorithms and Geometry’ in March 2024, which was funded by German Science\nL45@P0: Foundation grant 522790373.\nL46@P0-1: 12 MATCHINGS IN HYPERCUBES EXTEND TO LONG CYCLES\nL47@P1: M′\nL48@P1: (d) M′\nL49@P1: (e) M′(f) M′\nL50@P1: (c) (a) Q4\nL51@P1: ∅\nL52@P1: 2\nL53@P1: 3\nL54@P1: 12 23 123 4\nL55@P1: 124 1234 34 M\nL56@P1: (b) 13 134 1 14 24234\nL57@P1: Figure 1. Cycles and matchings in the hypercube Q4: (a) a Hamilton cycle, where the\nL58@P1: vertex labels omit curly brackets and commas for conciseness; (b) a perfect matching M\nL59@P1: and an extension of M to a Hamilton cycle; (c) a maximal, but not perfect matching M′;\nL60@P1: (d) an extension of M′to a cycle factor with two cycles; (e) an extension of M′to a\nL61@P1: (non-Hamilton) cycle; (f) an extension of M′to a Hamilton cycle, obtained by joining the\nL62@P1: two cycles in (d).\nL63@P1: Hamilton cycles. This proof technique has been exploited in several subsequent papers (see\nL64@P1: e.g. [Gre09, GNŠ18, AAA+15]), and we will also use it heavily in our arguments.\nL65@P1: Clearly, not every matching in Qd is perfect or extends to a perfect matching. In other words,\nL66@P1: there are (inclusion-)maximal matchings in Qd that are not perfect; see Figure 1 (c). Therefore,\nL67@P1: Theorem 2 leaves open the question whether every (not necessarily perfect) matching extends to\nL68@P1: a Hamilton cycle; see Figure 1 (f). Dvořák and Fink [DF19] obtained some positive evidence for\nL69@P1: small matchings.\nL70@P1: Theorem 3 ([DF19]). Every matching of Qd, d ≥ 2, with at most d\nL71@P1: 2/16 + d/4 edges can be\nL72@P1: extended to a Hamilton cycle.\nL73@P1: Another relaxation of the Ruskey-Savage conjecture, proposed by Vandenbussche and West [VW13],\nL74@P1: is to consider extensions to a cycle factor, i.e., a collection of disjoint cycles that together visit\nL75@P1: all vertices of the graph; see Figure 1 (d). This variant of the problem was also settled by\nL76@P1: Fink [Fin19].\nL77@P1-2: Theorem 4 ([Fin19]). Every matching of Qd, d ≥ 2, can be extended to a cycle factor.MATCHINGS IN HYPERCUBES EXTEND TO LONG CYCLES 3\nL78@P2: 1.1. Our results. In this paper we prove that every matching of Qd extends to a single cycle C.\nL79@P2: However, C possibly omits some vertices of Qd, i.e., C is not necessarily a Hamilton cycle; see\nL80@P2: Figure 1 (e). Our theorem also holds in the stronger setting of the complete graph K(Qd).\nL81@P2: Theorem 5. Every matching of K(Qd), d ≥ 2, can be extended to a cycle.\nL82@P2: Furthermore, we can give some guarantee about the length of the cycle obtained from our\nL83@P2: construction, namely that it visits a constant fraction of the vertex set V (Qd). This follows by\nL84@P2: using that any matching can be extended to a matching that contains a constant fraction of all\nL85@P2: vertices.\nL86@P2: Theorem 6. Every matching of Qd, d ≥ 2, can be extended to a cycle of length at least 2\nL87@P2: 3\nL88@P2: |V (Qd)| =\nL89@P2: 2\nL90@P2: d+1/3.\nL91@P2: Theorem 7. Every matching of K(Qd), d ≥ 2, can be extended to a cycle of length at\nL92@P2: least 1\nL93@P2: 2\nL94@P2: |V (Qd)| = 2d−1.\nL95@P2: Our results are another step towards the Ruskey-Savage conjecture, and we want to point out\nL96@P2: an analogy to the middle levels conjecture, which asserts that the subgraph of Q2d+1 induced\nL97@P2: by all sets of size d or d + 1 admits a Hamilton cycle. Historically, Felsner and Trotter [FT95],\nL98@P2: Savage and Winkler [SW95], and Johnson [Joh04] first established the existence of long cycles in\nL99@P2: this graph, and their work subsequently led to a proof of the middle levels conjecture [Müt16].\nL100@P2: We also note that not every matching of K(Qd) can be extended to a Hamilton cycle, nor a\nL101@P2: cycle factor. In fact, any matching in K(Qd) between all vertices of the same parity has the\nL102@P2: property that any cycle extending it includes only half of the vertices from the other parity, so it\nL103@P2: has only length 3\nL104@P2: 4\nL105@P2: |V (Qd)|. The parity of a vertex u of Qn is the parity of |u|, i.e., of the size\nL106@P2: of the set u. In particular, in K(Q2), the matching with the single edge (∅, {1, 2}) can only be\nL107@P2: extended to a cycle of length 3.\nL108@P2: Clearly, if a matching M of K(Qd) is extendable to a Hamilton cycle, then M has the same\nL109@P2: number of vertices of each parity. Note that every matching of B(Qd) satisfies this condition,\nL110@P2: where B(Qd) is the complete bipartite graph obtained from Qd by adding all edges between\nL111@P2: vertices of opposite parity. However, Dvořák and Fink [DF19] showed that this condition is\nL112@P2: not sufficient, by constructing a matching of B(Qd) for d ≥ 9 which cannot be extended to a\nL113@P2: Hamilton cycle, nor a cycle factor.\nL114@P2: To prove Theorem 5 inductively, we use the following auxiliary theorem, which yields a\nL115@P2: cycle that extends a given matching but also avoids one ‘forbidden’ vertex z, i.e., avoiding z\nL116@P2: is an additional constraint imposed on the cycle. Using that Qd is vertex-transitive, we may\nL117@P2: assume w.l.o.g. that the forbidden vertex is z = ∅. The following theorem requires some\nL118@P2: additional conditions, and to state them we need to introduce some notation: We split Qd in\nL119@P2: a direction i ∈ [d] into subgraphs Qi\nL120@P2: 0\nL121@P2: and Qi\nL122@P2: 1\nL123@P2: , where Qi\nL124@P2: 1\nL125@P2: is the subgraph induced by vertices\nL126@P2: of Qd containing i and Qi\nL127@P2: 0\nL128@P2: is the subgraph induced by vertices of Qd not containing i. Note\nL129@P2: that z ∈ V (Qi\nL130@P2: 0\nL131@P2: ) for every i ∈ [d]. The set of edges of Qd that have one end vertex in Qi\nL132@P2: 0\nL133@P2: and\nL134@P2: the other in Qi\nL135@P2: 1\nL136@P2: is called a layer in direction i. This set splits into two equal-sized sets of edges,\nL137@P2: depending on the parity of their end vertices in Qi\nL138@P2: 0\nL139@P2: , and we refer to each of these two sets of\nL140@P2: edges as a half-layer. In this way, all d2\nL141@P2: d−1\nL142@P2: edges of Qd are partitioned into d many layers each\nL143@P2: of cardinality 2\nL144@P2: d−1\nL145@P2: , or 2d many half-layers, each of cardinality 2\nL146@P2: d−2\nL147@P2: .\nL148@P2: Theorem 8. Let d ≥ 5. A matching M of K(Qd) that avoids z = ∅ can be extended to a cycle\nL149@P2: that avoids z if and only if it satisfies the following condition:\nL150@P2: (H) for every i ∈ [d], if M contains a half-layer in direction i, then there is a vertex u ∈ V (Qi\nL151@P2: 0\nL152@P2: )\\{z}\nL153@P2: that is avoided by M.\nL154@P2: We remark that the statement of Theorem 8 is true for d = 2 and d = 3, but false for d = 4,\nL155@P2-3: and all counterexamples can be seen in [GNŠ18, Figs. 7–9].4 MATCHINGS IN HYPERCUBES EXTEND TO LONG CYCLES\nL156@P3: In the following we will repeatedly refer to the condition on M stated in Theorem 8 as\nL157@P3: property (H). Note that this property depends on Qd, M and z, but for simplicity we do not\nL158@P3: make this dependence explicit, as the context should always be clear later when we apply the\nL159@P3: theorem to subcubes Qi\nL160@P3: 0\nL161@P3: and Qi\nL162@P3: 1\nL163@P3: for some i ∈ [d], certain matchings in those subcubes, and\nL164@P3: certain vertices to avoid in them. Note that the vertex u must have the same (even) parity as z,\nL165@P3: as all vertices in Qi\nL166@P3: 0\nL167@P3: of the opposite (odd) parity are covered by the half-layer.\nL168@P3: We show that Theorem 5 follows from Theorem 8 for any fixed dimension d ≥ 5, and we prove\nL169@P3: Theorem 8 by induction on d. The base case d = 5 for Theorem 8 is settled with computer help.\nL170@P3: 1.2. Applications to Hamilton-laceability. The hypercube Qd is Hamilton-laceable, i.e., it\nL171@P3: admits a Hamilton path between any two prescribed end vertices of opposite parity [Sim78].\nL172@P3: Gregor, Novotný, and Škrekovski [GNŠ18] considered laceability combined with matching\nL173@P3: extensions. Specifically, they considered the problem of extending a perfect matching of Qd to a\nL174@P3: Hamilton path between two prescribed end vertices with opposite parity. Their proof works in\nL175@P3: the more general setting of the complete bipartite graph B(Qd). For a matching M of Qd and\nL176@P3: one of its vertices x, we write xM for the other end vertex of the edge of M incident with x.\nL177@P3: Theorem 9 ([GNŠ18, Thm. 2]). Let d ≥ 5, and let x, y be two vertices of opposite parity in Qd.\nL178@P3: A perfect matching M of B(Qd) with xy /∈ M can be extended to a Hamilton path with end\nL179@P3: vertices x and y if and only if (M \\ {xxM, yyM}) ∪ {xMyM} contains no half-layers.\nL180@P3: The following is an equivalent reformulation of this theorem.\nL181@P3: Theorem 10 ([GNŠ18, Thm. 3]). Let d ≥ 5, and let x, y be two vertices of opposite parity in Qd.\nL182@P3: A perfect matching M of B(Qd \\ {x, y}) can be extended to a cycle that avoids x and y if and\nL183@P3: only if M contains no half-layers.\nL184@P3: In this theorem, Qd \\ {x, y} represents the graph obtained from Qd by removing the two\nL185@P3: vertices x and y.\nL186@P3: The authors also conjectured the strengthenings of Theorems 9 and 10 obtained by replacing\nL187@P3: B(Qd) and B(Qd \\ {x, y}) by K(Qd) and K(Qd \\ {x, y}), respectively. In fact, these stronger\nL188@P3: versions follow easily from our Theorem 8, settling the conjecture raised by Gregor, Novotný,\nL189@P3: and Škrekovski.\nL190@P3: Theorem 11. Let d ≥ 5, and let x, y be two vertices of opposite parity in Qd. A perfect\nL191@P3: matching M of K(Qd) with xy /∈ M can be extended to a Hamilton path with end vertices x\nL192@P3: and y if and only if (M \\ {xxM, yyM}) ∪ {xMyM} contains no half-layers.\nL193@P3: We prove the following equivalent reformulation of this theorem.\nL194@P3: Theorem 12. Let d ≥ 5, and let x, y be two vertices of opposite parity in Qd. A perfect\nL195@P3: matching M of K(Qd \\ {x, y}) can be extended to a cycle that avoids x and y if and only if M\nL196@P3: contains no half-layers.\nL197@P3: 1.3. Avoiding and including other structures. In the literature, there has also been\nL198@P3: substantial work on extending sets of edges E other than matchings to Hamilton cycles. In\nL199@P3: addition, there is interest in avoiding certain sets F of ‘forbidden’ edges. These questions are\nL200@P3: motivated by applications in computer networks, where the hypercube is frequently used as a\nL201@P3: network topology with a number of desirable properties, such as small degree and diameter. In\nL202@P3: this context, including or avoiding certain edges corresponds to prescribed connections or faulty\nL203@P3: connections, respectively.\nL204@P3: Dvořák [Dvo05] showed that for any set E of at most 2d − 3 edges in Qd, d ≥ 2, that form\nL205@P3: disjoint paths, there is a Hamilton cycle that contains all of E; see Figure 2 (a). Dvořák and\nL206@P3: Gregor [DG07] proved that for any set E of at most 2d − 4 edges in Qd, d ≥ 5, that form disjoint\nL207@P3-4: paths and two vertices x and y of opposite parity that are neither internal vertices of the pathsMATCHINGS IN HYPERCUBES EXTEND TO LONG CYCLES 5\nL208@P4: E\nL209@P4: (a) F(b) F\nL210@P4: (d) M(c)\nL211@P4: Figure 2. Illustration of Hamilton cycles in Qd, d = 4, subject to various constraints:\nL212@P4: (a) a set E of 2d − 3 = 5 edges and a Hamilton cycle extending it; (b) a set F of 2d − 5 = 3\nL213@P4: edges and a Hamilton cycle avoiding it; (c) a perfect matching M and a Hamilton cycle\nL214@P4: avoiding it; (d) a set F of\nL215@P4: d\nL216@P4: 2\nL217@P4: \u0001\nL218@P4: − 2 = 4 vertices and a cycle of length 2\nL219@P4: d − 2|F| = 8 avoiding\nL220@P4: it.\nL221@P4: nor end vertices of the same path, there is a Hamilton path with end vertices x and y that\nL222@P4: contains all of E.\nL223@P4: We now consider the problem of avoiding a set F of ‘forbidden’ edges. In this direction,\nL224@P4: Latifi, Zheng and Bagherzadeh [LZB92] showed that for any set F of at most d − 2 edges in Qd,\nL225@P4: there is a Hamilton cycle that avoids F. Chan and Lee [CL91] proved that for any set F of at\nL226@P4: most 2d − 5 edges in Qd, d ≥ 3, such that every vertex of Qd is incident to at least two edges not\nL227@P4: in F, there is a Hamilton cycle that avoids F (see also [LW14]); see Figure 2 (b). With regards\nL228@P4: to perfect matchings, Dimitrov, Dvořák, Gregor and Škrekovski [DDGŠ09] proved that for a\nL229@P4: given perfect matching M of Qd, there is a Hamilton cycle that avoids M if and only if Qd \\ M\nL230@P4: is a connected graph; see Figure 2 (c).\nL231@P4: Interestingly, the bounds 2d−3, 2d−4, d−2, 2d−5 in the aforementioned results on extending\nL232@P4: or avoiding certain sets of edges are all best possible, i.e., prescribing or forbidding more edges\nL233@P4: sometimes leads to situations where the desired Hamilton cycle or path does not exist.\nL234@P4: Instead of forbidding edges, one may also consider the problem of forbidding certain vertices.\nL235@P4: To this end, Fink and Gregor [FG12] proved that for any set F of at most\nL236@P4: d\nL237@P4: 2\nL238@P4: \u0001\nL239@P4: − 2 vertices of Qd,\nL240@P4: d ≥ 3, there is a cycle of length at least 2\nL241@P4: d − 2|F| that avoids all vertices in F; see Figure 2 (d).\nL242@P4: Their result answers a conjecture by Castañeda and Gotchev [CG10], and the bound\nL243@P4: d\nL244@P4: 2\nL245@P4: \u0001\nL246@P4: − 2\nL247@P4: is best possible. Note that if we forbid some number of vertices of the same parity, then any\nL248@P4: extending cycle will also skip the same number of vertices of the opposite parity. This explains\nL249@P4: that we need to allow skipping 2|F| many vertices instead of only |F|.\nL250@P4: 1.4. Other restricted Gray codes. Hamilton cycles and paths in the hypercube are often\nL251@P4: referred to as Gray codes [Müt23, Sav97]. In this setting, we view the vertices of the hypercube\nL252@P4: as bitstrings of length n, given by the indicator vectors of the corresponding subsets. The\nL253@P4: well-known binary reflected Gray code, named after the Bell Labs researcher Frank Gray, is\nL254@P4: a particularly elegant construction of such a cycle that lends itself well to fast algorithmic\nL255@P4: computation, optimally so that each bit flipped is computed in constant time. In addition to\nL256@P4-5: the work discussed in the preceding section about Gray codes that include or avoid certain6 MATCHINGS IN HYPERCUBES EXTEND TO LONG CYCLES\nL257@P5: substructures, there has been a large amount of work on Gray codes that satisfy various other\nL258@P5: kinds of constraints, motivated by different applications:\nL259@P5: • balanced Gray codes [BS96], where each bit is flipped equally often;\nL260@P5: • non-local Gray codes [Ram90], where any 2\nL261@P5: t\nL262@P5: consecutive bitstrings flip more than t distinct\nL263@P5: bits, for every 2 ≤ t ≤ n − 1;\nL264@P5: • run-length restricted Gray codes [GG03], where any two consecutive flips of the same bit are\nL265@P5: almost n steps apart;\nL266@P5: • antipodal Gray codes [KS04], where the complement of any bitstring x is visited exactly n\nL267@P5: steps before or after x;\nL268@P5: • monotone Gray codes [SW95], where the Hamming weight of the bitstrings never decreases\nL269@P5: by more than 2 from what it was before along the code;\nL270@P5: • transition restricted Gray codes [BR96, DDGŠ13], where the pairs of consecutively flipped\nL271@P5: bits are edges of a certain transition graph;\nL272@P5: • Beckett-Gray codes [SW07], where on each transition 1 → 0 the least recently entered 1-bit\nL273@P5: has to be flipped;\nL274@P5: • single-track Gray codes [Etz07, GMM24], where each bit follows the same cyclically shifted\nL275@P5: flipping pattern along the code.\nL276@P5: 1.5. Outline of this paper. In Section 2 we present some notations and terminology used\nL277@P5: throughout this paper, as well as some auxiliary lemmas needed later. In Sections 3 and 4 we\nL278@P5: present the proofs of Theorems 5 and 8, respectively. Section 5 contains the proofs of Theorems 6,\nL279@P5: 7, and 12. We conclude with some open problems in Section 6.\nL280@P5: 2. Preliminaries\nL281@P5: We need the following definitions and auxiliary lemmas.\nL282@P5: 2.1. Notation and definitions. For a graph G, the sets of vertices and edges of G are denoted\nL283@P5: by V (G) and E(G), respectively. Furthermore, we write K(G) for the complete graph on the\nL284@P5: vertex set of G. If G is connected and bipartite, then there is a unique bipartition of its vertices,\nL285@P5: and we write B(G) for the complete bipartite graph with that bipartition. In other words, K(G)\nL286@P5: is obtained from G by adding all missing edges to G, and B(G) is obtained from G by adding\nL287@P5: all possible edges to G, while preserving the property that the graph remains bipartite.\nL288@P5: For two sets A and B, the symmetric difference of A and B is denoted by A △ B. Note that\nL289@P5: Qd has an edge between any two vertices u and v with |u △ v| = 1. For a vertex u of Qd and\nL290@P5: i ∈ [d], we define u\nL291@P5: i\nL292@P5: := u △ {i}, and we refer to i as the direction of the edge uui. We say that u\nL293@P5: is even or odd, if |u| is even or odd, respectively.\nL294@P5: A linear forest is collection of vertex-disjoint paths. The terminals of a linear forest are its\nL295@P5: vertices of degree 1, i.e., the end vertices of the paths. A shortcut of a linear forest in a graph G\nL296@P5: is the set of edges of K(G) that connect the two terminals of every path to each other.\nL297@P5: For a graph G and a subset of edges M of G, we say that a vertex u of G is covered by M, if\nL298@P5: u is an end vertex of one of the edges of M. We write V (M) for the set of all vertices covered\nL299@P5: by M. If u is not covered by M we say that it is avoided by M. Recall that for a vertex u\nL300@P5: covered by an edge of a matching M, we write uM for the other end vertex of that edge of M.\nL301@P5: Furthermore, for i ∈ [d] we use the abbreviation u\nL302@P5: iM := (ui\nL303@P5: )M.\nL304@P5: For a set of edges F ⊆ E(K(Qd)) and a direction i ∈ [d] we define F\nL305@P5: i\nL306@P5: 0\nL307@P5: := F ∩ E(K(Qi\nL308@P5: 0\nL309@P5: )) and\nL310@P5: F\nL311@P5: i\nL312@P5: 1\nL313@P5: := F ∩ E(K(Qi\nL314@P5: 1\nL315@P5: )), and we write F\nL316@P5: i\nL317@P5: − for the edges of F joining vertices of Qi\nL318@P5: 0\nL319@P5: and Qi\nL320@P5: 1\nL321@P5: . This\nL322@P5: partitions F into three disjoint sets F\nL323@P5: i\nL324@P5: 0\nL325@P5: , F\nL326@P5: i\nL327@P5: 1\nL328@P5: and F\nL329@P5: i\nL330@P5: −.\nL331@P5: The following definitions are illustrated in Figure 3. Recall the definition of half-layers given\nL332@P5: in Section 1.1. Given some direction i ∈ [d], a half-layer in Qi\nL333@P5: 0\nL334@P5: or in Qi\nL335@P5: 1\nL336@P5: is referred to as a\nL337@P5: quad-layer in Qd. Clearly, a half-layer has 2\nL338@P5: d−2\nL339@P5: edges, and a quad-layer has 2\nL340@P5: d−3\nL341@P5: edges. A near\nL342@P5: half-layer (near quad-layer) is a half-layer (quad-layer) minus one edge. We refer to the two\nL343@P5-6: end vertices of the removed edge, which are not part of the near half-layer (near quad-layer), asMATCHINGS IN HYPERCUBES EXTEND TO LONG CYCLES 7\nL344@P6: (a) (b) (c) Qi\nL345@P6: 0 Qi1 Qi0 Qi1\nL346@P6: Qi\nL347@P6: 0 Qi1\nL348@P6: i i\nL349@P6: i\nL350@P6: e1 e2 e3\nL351@P6: v1\nL352@P6: v2\nL353@P6: Figure 3. The red edges in Q4 form a (a) half-layer, (b) near half-layer with extension\nL354@P6: vertices v1 and v2, or (c) matching that includes the covered near half-layer {e1, e2, e3}.\nL355@P6: The subset of red edges incident with a vertex in Qi\nL356@P6: 1\nL357@P6: form a (a) quad-layer, (b) near\nL358@P6: quad-layer, or (c) matching that includes the covered near quad-layer {e3}. Every vertex x\nL359@P6: in (a) and (b) colored blue is one for which the (near) quad-layer contained in Qi\nL360@P6: 1\nL361@P6: is\nL362@P6: x-dangerous. The dashed vertical line separates Qi\nL363@P6: 0\nL364@P6: and Qi\nL365@P6: 1\nL366@P6: .\nL367@P6: extension vertices of the near half-layer (near quad-layer). A 2-near half-layer is a half-layer\nL368@P6: minus two edges. We refer to the four end vertices of the removed edges, which are not part of\nL369@P6: the 2-near half-layer, as extension vertices of the 2-near half layer. Given a matching M that\nL370@P6: includes a near half-layer (near quad-layer), we say that this near half-layer (near quad-layer) is\nL371@P6: covered if both of its extension vertices are covered by two distinct edges of M.\nL372@P6: A half-layer of Qd is x-dangerous for every vertex x not incident with any edge from the\nL373@P6: half-layer. An x-dangerous near half-layer is an x-dangerous half-layer minus one edge. Note\nL374@P6: that x cannot be one of the extension vertices of the near half-layer. For a vertex x of Qd, a\nL375@P6: quad-layer is x-dangerous if x is not incident with any edge from the quad-layer and x belongs to\nL376@P6: the same (d − 1)-dimensional subcube as the quad-layer. Furthermore, we define an x-dangerous\nL377@P6: near quad-layer as an x-dangerous quad-layer minus one edge. Similarly to before, x cannot be\nL378@P6: one of the extension vertices of the near quad-layer.\nL379@P6: 2.2. A cycle that avoids two vertices. The first lemma asserts that a cycle that extends an\nL380@P6: almost perfect matching of K(Qd) and avoids a particular vertex x is forced to also avoid another\nL381@P6: vertex y, provided that x and y have the correct parity. This is useful when applying induction\nL382@P6: in the proof of Theorem 8, because it prevents that the invariant ‘cycle avoids one prescribed\n"
